1.3 Measuring prediction error
To find the best hyperplane we must first quantify error. Write the residual on example \(i\) as \(\residualpart{e_i}=\targetpart{y_i}-\predictionpart{\hat y_i}\). The standard choice for regression is the mean squared error (MSE):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\loss(\parameterpart{\vect{w}})
&= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\residualpart{e_i^2}
= \frac{1}{n}\norm{\residualpart{\vect{e}}}_2^2, \\
\residualpart{\vect{e}}
&= \targetpart{\vect{y}}
-\featurepart{\matr{X}}\parameterpart{\vect{w}}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{1.1}\]
The dark red residual vector \(\residualpart{\vect{e}}\) records the discrepancy on each example. Squaring and averaging the residuals turns them into one number, the objective that training minimizes. The maximum-likelihood section explains why squared error is a principled choice.
The loss is a function of the parameters. With one weight and one bias, \(\parameterpart{\vect{w}} = (w,b)\), the MSE is a surface over the \((w,b)\) plane, called the loss landscape. Figure 1.4, later in this chapter, draws it as a surface and as contour lines for a small dataset. Learning means finding a low point on this surface. The next two methods differ in whether they solve for that point directly or approach it step by step.

1.9 Okay, so: the simplest model establishes the learning framework
Learning a function from examples used these components:

A model family: hyperplanes parameterized by knobs \(\vect{w}, b\), with the bias absorbed into the weight vector when compact notation helps.
A loss: MSE, which is maximum likelihood under Gaussian noise and converts prediction error into the scalar signal that guides every update.
A fitting procedure: solve least squares directly, or approach its solution through gradient updates.
The generalization lens: bias vs. variance, the data splits that referee them, and regularization as a first countermeasure to overfitting.

One reframing will carry the whole book. A neuron computes the same weighted sum and then applies a fixed function \(\sigma\), its activation. Linear regression is the neuron whose activation is the identity, \(\sigma(z) = z\):
\[
\underbrace{
  \predictionpart{\hat y}
  =\parameterpart{\vect{w}^\top}\featurepart{\vect{x}}+\parameterpart{b}
}_{\text{this chapter}}
\quad \xrightarrow{\ \text{add a nonlinearity}\ } \quad
\underbrace{
  \predictionpart{\hat y}
  =\sigma\!\left(
    \parameterpart{\vect{w}^\top}\featurepart{\vect{x}}+\parameterpart{b}
  \right)
}_{\text{a neuron}} .
\]
First, in Chapter 2, a sigmoid activation turns our regressor into a classifier. Then, in Chapter 3, we stack neurons and discover why the nonlinearity is not optional.






TipThe first “make it learnable” step



We first saw the geometry of the linear response in Figure 1.2. Now we can name its neural-network form: a neuron with the identity activation. The model–loss–gradient–update pattern carries over, while the particular loss and derivatives change with the model. The nonlinearity is the first ingredient that expands what the model can learn.

16.5 From encoder–decoder translation to a causal language model
The original Transformer of Vaswani et al. was an encoder–decoder architecture designed for bilingual translation. Its encoder stack uses bidirectional (unmasked) self-attention to construct contextual representations of the source sequence. Its decoder stack applies causal self-attention over the target prefix, followed by cross-attention whose queries originate in the decoder while keys and values are retrieved from the encoder memory:
\[
\begin{array}{lll}
\text{encoder:}& \matr{X}_s \to \text{self-attention} \to \text{FFN},\\
\text{decoder:}& \matr{X}_t \to \text{causal self-attention}
\to \text{cross-attention to encoder} \to \text{FFN}.
\end{array}
\]
The book-corpus task of Chapter 12 requires autoregressive next-character prediction from preceding context alone. Because there is no external source sequence, both the encoder stack and the cross-attention sublayers are unnecessary. The resulting architecture is a decoder-only causal Transformer that consists of token and positional embeddings, two pre-LayerNorm blocks, a final layer normalization, and a linear projection to vocabulary logits. These logits feed directly into the cross-entropy loss. Softmax normalization belongs inside attention routing and autoregressive sampling, not between the output projection and the loss.
